course: biology
lesson: 144
title: Babe: real pigs, sheep and sheepdogs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Babe, a polite little pig learns to herd sheep and wins a sheepdog trial, while a duck crows at dawn to be useful and the farm cat sneers.
explain: Real herding is a dog's job: sheepdogs come from wolves, which hunt by circling and driving prey, so a Border collie crouches, creeps and stares, and the frightened sheep bunch up and move away from it.
explain: Babe's cleverness is real, though: pigs solve puzzles as well as many dogs, remember well and can even use a mirror to find hidden food.
explain: Picture a hillside: a shepherd whistles, the dog swings left round the flock, and three hundred sheep move as one. Calm, slow work keeps them steady, which is what Babe's politeness gets right.
fact: Pigs are not used for herding, but they are clever and can be trained with rewards to fetch, sit and come when called.
fact: Pigs learn tricks and solve puzzles as well as many dogs, and they can use a mirror to find food hidden behind them.
fact: A Border collie moves sheep by crouching, creeping and staring; this hunting stare is called «eye» and sheep move away from it.
fact: A Border collie called Chaser learned the names of over 1,000 toys.
fact: Shepherds use whistles and calls for left, right, stop and come in, because a whistle carries farther than a voice over hills and wind; «come by» means clockwise round the flock and «away to me» anticlockwise.
fact: Scared sheep bunch tightly together, because the middle of a crowd is safer, and that is what lets one dog move hundreds of sheep.
fact: Calm, slow handling keeps sheep less stressed; stressed sheep scatter and can hurt themselves.
fact: Ducks cannot crow; roosters crow, especially before dawn, using an inner body clock, and the top rooster usually crows first.
fact: Real farm cats are useful mousers that keep mice and rats away from barns full of grain.
fact: Sheepdog trials are real competitions where dog and shepherd move sheep through gates and into a pen.
fact: Sheep follow a leader one behind another along familiar paths, which make stripes on old grassy hillsides.
fact: Sheep see about 300 degrees around them, with a small blind gap right behind them, and see less well right in front of their noses, so a dog coming straight from behind may surprise them.
exercise: Are pigs used to herd sheep in real life? | No, but they are clever and can learn tasks with rewards | Yes, pigs are used to herd sheep on most sheep farms in the world | Yes, but only in Australia | No, pigs cannot learn anything at all
exercise: How clever are pigs? | They solve puzzles as well as many dogs | They are the least clever farm animal | They can learn only one trick in life | They are cleverer than any person
exercise: What is a sheepdog's «eye»? | A fixed, crouching stare that makes sheep move away | A special third eye on its forehead | The dog's habit of winking at the shepherd so the sheep know to move | The dog closing its eyes to listen
exercise: Why do sheepdogs herd so well? | They come from wolves, which hunt by circling and driving prey | They were taught by the sheep long ago, when dogs and sheep lived together | They love the smell of wool | They are scared of being alone
exercise: Why does a shepherd use whistles? | Whistles carry farther than voices over hills and wind | Whistles hurt the sheep's ears, so they run away from the sound | The dog cannot hear a person's voice at all, only high sounds | Whistles make the sheep calm and sleepy, so they are easy to move
exercise: What does «come by» tell a sheepdog? | Go clockwise round the flock | Go anticlockwise round the flock | Lie down flat and stay very still | Come straight back to the shepherd
exercise: Why do sheep bunch together when a dog circles? | The middle of a crowd is safer from predators | They are cold and want to keep warm, even on hot summer days | They are trying to trap the dog | The dog pulls them with its teeth
exercise: Why is calm, slow handling better for sheep? | Stressed sheep scatter and can hurt themselves | Calm sheep grow more wool at once | Slow work makes sheep run faster | Sheep fall asleep when it is quiet and are then easy to lift
exercise: In Babe a duck crows at dawn. What is real? | Ducks cannot crow; roosters crow before dawn | Ducks crow louder than roosters | All farm birds crow at dawn | Only female ducks crow
exercise: How does a rooster know morning is coming? | It has an inner body clock | It sees the sunrise every time | The farmer wakes it up every morning | The other birds wake it with their songs
exercise: Is a real farm cat mean and sneaky like in Babe? | No, it is a useful mouser that guards the grain | Yes, it attacks pigs and dogs | Yes, it steals eggs from the hens every day and scares the chicks | No, farm cats are scared of mice
exercise: What is a sheepdog trial? | A competition moving sheep through gates and into a pen | A test of how fast a dog can run alone across a big field | A court case about a naughty dog that chased the farmer's sheep | A show where dogs are judged on their looks and their coats
exercise: How do sheep walk along a narrow path? | In a long line, following a leader | All side by side in a row | Backwards, watching behind them | Each one alone, far apart
exercise: About how wide is a sheep's view? | About 300 degrees | Only about 30 degrees | Only about 90 degrees | Exactly 360 degrees
exercise: Where does a sheep see least well? | Right in front of its nose | Far away on the sides | High up in the sky | In bright sunshine
exercise: How many toy names did the Border collie Chaser learn? | Over 1,000 | Only about 10 | Only about 100 | About 100,000
test: «nan's farm: 2 dogs, 4 pigs and 1 duck, how many legs in the yard» | 26 | 28 | 24 | 7
test: «pigs are thick, they just roll in mud all day» What is right? | Pigs are clever, solving puzzles as well as many dogs | Yes, pigs cannot learn anything | Yes, pigs are the slowest learners on a farm and never learn tricks | No, pigs are clever only when they are piglets
test: In a cartoon, a sheepdog barks nonstop and chases sheep in every direction. What do real good sheepdogs do? | Work quietly, crouching and staring so the sheep move steadily | Bark as loudly as they can all the time | Bite the sheep to make them run | Run round and round in circles until the sheep get dizzy and fall over
test: What would happen if sheep did not bunch together when scared? | One dog could not easily move hundreds of sheep | Sheep would be safer from wolves | The dog would herd them faster | Nothing at all would change
test: «sod the whistle, just shout at the dog, it's 2 fields away» Why do shepherds whistle anyway? | A whistle carries farther over hills and wind | Dogs hate the sound of voices | Shouting makes dogs run away | Whistles are a law for shepherds
test: «my mate says the cock crows coz it sees the sun come up» What is right? | It crows by an inner body clock, even before it sees the sun | Yes, it crows only at the moment when the sunlight first hits its eyes | Yes, it cannot crow on cloudy days | No, it crows because it is hungry
test: Babe could find a hidden apple by looking in a mirror. Could a real pig? | Yes, pigs have learned to use a mirror to find hidden food | No, pigs cannot see in mirrors | No, pigs always think the mirror shows another pig and try to fight it | Only after years of training every day
test: A dog runs straight at a flock from behind and the sheep jump in surprise. Why? | Sheep see least right behind and right in front | Sheep cannot hear at all | Sheep sleep with their eyes open | Sheep see only what is in front of them
test: «why are there stripes across that old hill, like steps» What made them? | Sheep walking one behind another on the same paths | Giant worms digging in the soil | Farmers ploughing the side of the hill into steps every single year | Rain washing the grass into lines
test: «mean old barn cat, why do farmers even keep one» Why do they? | It catches mice and rats that come for the grain | It guards the farm from foxes | It herds the hens at night | It warns the farmer when it rains
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the employment of pigs in the herding of sheep. | Pigs are not so employed, although they are intelligent and may be trained to various tasks by reward | Pigs are customarily employed for this purpose on most sheep farms | Pigs are incapable of acquiring any trained behaviour | Pigs surpass dogs in this employment and have largely replaced them on the farms of Britain and Australia
exam: Identify the principal reason for which sheep congregate closely when circled by a dog. | A position within the group affords greater safety from predators | The animals seek warmth from one another | The animals endeavour to entrap the dog | The animals are physically drawn together by the dog, which pulls them with its teeth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the crowing of the domestic fowl at dawn? | It is performed by the male fowl in accordance with an internal clock, independently of the sunrise | It is performed by ducks in the absence of a rooster | It occurs exclusively upon the sun becoming visible | It is performed by any farm bird at all, but only upon the instruction of the farmer
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the command «come by» as employed by shepherds. | The dog is to proceed clockwise around the flock | The dog is to proceed anticlockwise around the flock | The dog is to lie down and remain stationary | The dog is to return directly to the shepherd
